## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multi-city data sovereignty, strict data contracts, and production readiness for evolving schemas at scale. It probes thinking on idempotent upserts, late-arriving data, GDPR deletion, and governance alongside trade-offs between Spark and Flink.

## Key Concepts
- City-level partitioning and data isolation
- Data contracts via schema registry and schema evolution
- Lakehouse upserts with Iceberg and idempotency guarantees
- Late data handling with watermarks and allowed lateness
- GDPR deletion via tombstones and per-record lineage
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs for latency and throughput
- Data-quality gates and contract testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo contract check for incoming event against schema registry
function validateEvent(event, schema) {
  // validate required fields and types; fail fast on mismatch
  // this is illustrative; integrate with a real registry in production
  if (!event.vehicle_id || !event.ts) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-city data masking while preserving analytics?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward/forward compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes multi-tenant governance, scale, streaming semantics, schema drift handling, and retention policies across a large lakehouse. It also tests trade-offs between Spark and Flink for high-volume streaming and the practicality of per-tenant partitioning and access control.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data contracts and lineage
- Exactly-once streaming and idempotent upserts
- Iceberg partitioning by tenant/region
- Schema drift handling and canonical schema
- Data retention, GDPR-style deletes, and privacy masking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate event against per-tenant contract before upsert
function process(event, tenant) {
  const contract = registry.get(tenant);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  iceberg.merge(event, canonicalKey, tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift across tenants over time?
- What metrics and alerts would you monitor for data quality and lineage?
